From: Datel OS
Subject: serialhdl: discard what the port received before the session starts

connect_uart() runs stk500v2_leave() on every port it opens: the rate goes
to 2400, then to 115200 for 150 ms, then back. A microcontroller that the
host did not reset (the host rebooted, Klipper was restarted) goes on
sending the reports of its last configuration at its own rate meanwhile,
and what it sends in those 150 ms arrives as noise. stk500v2_leave() reads
most of it away. What arrives between its last read and the change of the
rate back stays in the tty's buffer: the session finds five or six bytes
of noise in front of the next report and skips both, up to that report's
sync byte. bytes_invalid then reads 14 to 21 right after the connect, on a
link that is sound.

Measured on a Creality K1 (the main MCU on a UART at 230400 baud, four ADC
reports every 300 ms), Klipper stopped, klippy's own stk500v2_leave() on
the port and its framing check on what follows: 3 of 150 and 4 of 150
connects had such bytes under two serial drivers, and 21 to 24 of 100
with 20 ms more between that last read and the rate change, which is what
a busy host does to it while it boots.

After stk500v2_leave(), read the port until it has been quiet for ten
character times, 3 ms at least, and give up after 100 ms. The noise is
gone then. Waiting for a quiet line, where a flush at a fixed moment would
cut a report that is just arriving in two, also lets the session start
between two reports. A connect takes 3.5 ms longer as a rule and 11 ms at
most.

With this change, on the same printer: 0 of 400 connects with invalid
bytes, 0 of 100 with the 20 ms added, 0 of 100 beside two processes that
burn CPU. Klipper itself, restarted 20 times against microcontrollers that
kept running: bytes_invalid 0 on all three links every time.

diff --git a/klippy/serialhdl.py b/klippy/serialhdl.py
index 761a330..1747ef7 100644
--- a/klippy/serialhdl.py
+++ b/klippy/serialhdl.py
@@ -320,6 +320,7 @@ class SerialReader:
                 self.reactor.pause(self.reactor.monotonic() + 5.0)
                 continue
             stk500v2_leave(serial_dev, self.reactor)
+            discard_stale_input(serial_dev, self.reactor)
             ret = self._start_session(serial_dev)
             if ret:
                 break
@@ -559,6 +560,27 @@ def stk500v2_leave(ser, reactor):
     ser.baudrate = origbaud
 
 
+# Discard what a port received before a session starts on it
+def discard_stale_input(ser, reactor):
+    # A microcontroller that the host did not reset goes on sending the
+    # reports of its last configuration. What it sends while
+    # stk500v2_leave() holds the port at another rate arrives as noise, and
+    # a session would skip that noise together with the message after it,
+    # up to the next sync byte, and count both as invalid bytes. Read until
+    # the line has been quiet for ten character times (3 ms at least): the
+    # noise is gone then, and the session starts between two messages.
+    quiet_time = max(0.003, 100.0 / ser.baudrate)
+    end_time = reactor.monotonic() + 0.100
+    discarded = 0
+    while reactor.monotonic() < end_time:
+        reactor.pause(reactor.monotonic() + quiet_time)
+        data = ser.read(4096)
+        if not data:
+            break
+        discarded += len(data)
+    logging.debug("Discarded %d bytes received before the session", discarded)
+
+
